-- APPLIED 2026-10-10 via scripts/run-sql-file.mjs. Not in schema_migrations.
-- 102: budget-room sentences rewritten to the measured budget tier (101: regional research + queue budget-room pass C1-C4, 2026-10-10). Places in C1-C4 were searched for cheaper printed tariffs (dharamshala, rest house) and none were found.


BEGIN;
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS backups.prose_cost_20261010 (tbl text, col text, key text, old_value text, saved_at timestamptz default now());
INSERT INTO backups.prose_cost_20261010 (tbl, col, key, old_value) VALUES
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'sissu/5', $cst$May Sissu is the first taste of Lahaul for most visitors. The Atal Tunnel exit is dramatic — you emerge from fluorescent-lit tunnel into a valley of completely different character from the Kullu side. The waterfall is in full roar — mist from its base drenches anyone standing at the roadside viewpoint. The village is small (maybe 200 houses) but photogenic: prayer flags, Buddhist gompa, stone-and-wood houses against dramatic mountain backdrop. The Gondhla Fort (8km toward Keylong) is a lesser-known gem — a wooden tower fort similar to Kinnauri architecture. The Tandi confluence (20km) where the Chandra and Bhaga rivers meet is the traditional gateway to Lahaul. Hotels at around ₹3,500. May is the month when Sissu works as a destination, not just a tunnel exit stop.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'chakrata/8', $cst$The forests are at their most alive — orchids bloom on tree trunks, butterflies appear in clouds along sunny trail sections, and the air carries the deep petrichor scent that synthetic candles try to imitate. Tiger Falls is still formidable. The Deoban trail is muddy but passable for experienced hikers. This is when you start seeing early autumn mushrooms — locals forage chanterelles and morels along the forest floor. Homestay hosts cook with whatever the forest provides. Rates stay low (around ₹1,300/night) and the experience stays raw. August is the quiet before Chakrata remembers it has a tourist season.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'devprayag/11', $cst$November sangam is when photographers make the trip. The Bhagirathi runs emerald, the Alaknanda runs jade, and the boundary between them — visible from the suspension bridge, from the ghats, from the Raghunath temple terrace — is clean enough to photograph without filters. The water is achingly cold for ritual dips, but the handful of devotees who come dip regardless. The town's dharamshala chai, served in glass tumblers at the ghat edge, warms you while the river view does everything else. The drive from Rishikesh is fog-free and stunning in autumn light. Rooms: around ₹1,400/night.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'uttarkashi/12', $cst$December is Uttarkashi at its core: cold, clear, quiet, holy. The Kashi Vishwanath temple's winter aarti — a handful of devotees, the Bhagirathi audible in the background, mountain cold in the air — is Uttarkashi's essence. NIM winter courses train the next generation of Indian mountaineers on ice and snow. The town market sells winter provisions: rajma, rice, dried fruit, wool. Hotels at around ₹1,050 with room heaters. The Bhagirathi in December — low, clear, blue — is the prettiest version of a river that will become the Ganga. December Uttarkashi strips away the Char Dham circus and shows you why this town has been sacred for centuries: it sits at the gates of the Himalayas, and in December, the gates are quiet.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'rudraprayag/4', $cst$April is Rudraprayag's last calm month. The town is preparing — hotels painting, restaurants expanding menus, jeep operators servicing vehicles. The pre-season energy is exciting if you know what's coming. The confluence is at ideal viewing: full from early snowmelt, clear enough to see the colour difference. Ukhimath (41km), where Kedarnath's deity spends winter, has temple ceremonies preparing for the return journey. This is insider access to the Char Dham machinery. Hotel rates start at around ₹2,100 and climb through the month. The 2013 memorial site is reflective and important. Book May accommodation now if you're joining the pilgrimage season.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'guptkashi/9', $cst$Late September combines the best of multiple seasons: monsoon clarity, reduced crowds, open trails, and comfortable temperatures. The Kedarnath trek post-monsoon is the insider's timing — the trail is clear, wildflowers still line the upper reaches, and you can actually see the peaks that pilgrims in May and July only imagine through cloud. Helicopter services run normally after mid-September. The temple stays open and darshan takes 15-30 minutes, no queue management barriers needed. Guptkashi's bazaar feels human-scaled again. The Kalimath Shakti Pith, 10 km away, hosts Navratri celebrations in late September with genuine village festival energy — not tourist-packaged, just community worship. Room rates: around ₹2,700 easy availability. The Mandakini at post-monsoon levels is beautiful — full but not angry, clean and cold. This is genuinely the best month for combining Kedarnath pilgrimage with Guptkashi heritage in a single, calm trip.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'rudraprayag/1', $cst$January Rudraprayag rewards the curious transit traveller. The confluence viewing point (10-minute walk from town) shows the Mandakini and Alaknanda meeting in winter clarity — different temperatures, different mineral loads, visibly different colours merging. The Koteshwar Mahadev temple, carved into a riverside cave, is Rudraprayag's hidden gem — in January you'll share it with priests and local devotees only. The Jim Corbett connection is real: the man-eating leopard killed 125 people between 1918-1926, and Corbett's account is one of India's great adventure narratives. Read it before you visit. Hotels are basic (around ₹1,800) but functional. Rudraprayag is 141km from Rishikesh (5-6 hours by road) and works as an overnight stop on any Garhwal exploration.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'rudraprayag/10', $cst$October is Rudraprayag's jewel month. The confluence sparkles in post-monsoon clarity. The Mandakini runs from blue to green depending on the light. The Alaknanda is deeper blue from higher-altitude sources. Together they create a visible meeting line that photographs beautifully. The surrounding terraced hills are post-monsoon lush. Kedarnath temple's closing ceremony (mid-October to early November) means the last pilgrim wave passes through. After that, the town exhales into genuine calm. The Koteshwar temple in October light is sublime. Hotels are reasonable (around ₹2,850). This is when you understand why Rudraprayag is one of the Panch Prayag — five sacred confluences.$cst$);
